
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the reusable piece is [3mjust the rule-checking logic[0m, or [3malso the wiring[0m — the submit handler, reading
  values out of the DOM, and putting error messages on screen. Right now [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does all three inline and reports
  errors to [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m, so nothing visible exists to reuse.

  - [1mValidation core only[0m — a [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m function returning errors. Each form keeps its own submit handler
    and decides how to display errors. Cheap, testable in isolation, zero DOM assumptions. Cost: every new form
    re-writes the same ~10 lines of read-fields/handle-submit/show-errors glue, which is the part that actually rots.
  - [1mCore + DOM binding[0m — the above plus [38;5;153mbindForm(formEl, schema, onValid)[39m that wires submit, pulls values by input
    [38;5;153mid[39m/[38;5;153mname[39m, and renders messages. A new form becomes a schema plus one call. Cost: the binder makes choices for you
    about error markup and where messages go, and it's the harder half to test without a DOM.
  - [1mCore + binding + a rendering hook[0m — as above, but the caller can supply its own renderer. Buys per-form flexibility
    at the price of an extra concept in the API.

  Cheap to change later: adding rules to the library, changing error message text. Expensive to change later: the
  schema's shape, and whether callers get an errors object or a rendered UI — both get baked into every call site.

  [1mMy recommendation: core + DOM binding[0m, with the binder a thin separate file over the core. "Reusable across multiple
  forms" is only really paid off when adding a form is a schema plus one line; keeping the core pure and DOM-free in its
  own file preserves testability, and a rendering hook can be added later without breaking the default.

